% ============================================================================
% possessive-noun — an example noun and which possessive-noun category its
% possessive form falls into, in a sentence that shows it doing that job
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% A possessive noun shows ownership. English forms it three different ways
% depending on the base noun: a singular noun adds 's ("the dog's collar"),
% a regular plural noun (already ending in s) adds only an apostrophe ("the
% bottles' shelf"), and an irregular plural noun (one that doesn't end in s)
% adds 's just like a singular noun would ("the geese's flock"). Recall is a
% binding query:
%
%     ? possessive_noun(dog, $Category)
%       % singular_possessive
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `possessive_noun(word, category)` carrying the source citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the
% category WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains
% on any word the source does not classify this way. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a category and recall which example word it names:
%
%     ? possessive_noun($Word, plural_possessive)
%       % bottles
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `word` is an apostrophe-free atom label rather than the
% punctuated possessive spelling: ADJ atom labels cannot contain an
% apostrophe, so this table uses the same apostrophe-free atom-label
% discipline `contraction.adj` already established — `dog` stands for the
% base noun whose possessive form is "dog's." The header prose below quotes
% the ORIGINAL punctuated example sentences, so each mapping remains
% independently checkable against the cited page.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "Possessive Nouns: How to Use
% Them, With Examples" article, which gives a worked example sentence for
% each of the three possessive-noun categories covered here:
%
%     word (this table's atom label)   category               example sentence (verbatim)
%     --------------------------------  ---------------------  ------------------------------------------------
%     dog                                singular_possessive    "I adjusted the dog's collar."
%     bottles                            plural_possessive       "The glasses are under the bottles' shelf."
%     geese                              irregular_possessive    "We followed them back to the geese's flock."
%
% WebFetch-verified TWICE before writing. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources and the same
% source family already cited by `sentence-type.adj`/`part-of-speech.adj`/
% `contraction.adj`. A noun that is NOT one of these three — e.g. "cat," a
% real noun whose possessive is "cat's," but not one this specific cited
% page's table draws from — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a classification the source never
% gave. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table possessive_noun {
    columns word, category

    row (dog, singular_possessive)
    row (bottles, plural_possessive)
    row (geese, irregular_possessive)

    source "I adjusted the dog's collar."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/parts-of-speech/possessive-nouns/"
    trust consensus
}
